Z||000000||JohnnyK||05-01-2000||11:28 PM||||Got this one from Canadian Tire to finish this damn job..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyways, it's a fairly shiny silver/dark bluish, and I'm wondering if I should just leave it like that, or go over it a few times with some rust proofing paint? All the responses possible would be appreciated, because this oil pan has been the biggest pain of my life..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||fieroshop||05-02-2000||11:00 AM||||Just paint the stupid thing with engine paint and get on with your life.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||05-02-2000||04:56 PM||||You idiot.. I mean I don't know if it came pre rust proofed... Doesn't look like it did, so a helpful suggestion would have been better than a stupid one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||05-02-2000||05:18 PM||||C'mon guys.... Don't get your knickers in a twist.||||reg||13
Z||000004||Gokart||05-02-2000||09:11 PM||||I'd call Canadian Tire and ask what that stuff is on the pan. Maybe it's already weather-proofed? If you decide to paint over the stuff and it's not easy to get off, at least scuff it with sandpaper and prime it before painting.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||05-03-2000||10:11 AM||||Are we really going to get something like this started again?!  Dump some hormones.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-03-2000||10:44 AM||||If it's like most new pans there's just some preservative coating or oil on it to keep it from rusting in inventory. not a final finish. I usually sand blast them out side, easier than sanding, then prime and paint with engine paint. I don't do any thing to the inside but degrease it good to make sure there's no dirt stuck to anything.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||05-03-2000||04:01 PM||||It's got a shiny, almost plastic look on top of it... I think it should be ok without painting?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaveT||05-03-2000||07:35 PM||||Johnny:<BR>Is the pan you got a refurbished one?Or is it<BR>new? I know the refurbished ones are coated<BR>(grey)and they are ok to install without painting.||||reg||1
Z||000009||stimpy||05-03-2000||08:55 PM||||Johnny, I really don't think fieroshop meant any insult. I have met George on several occaisions and he is one of the nicest guys you would hope to meet. Not to mention extremely knowledgable when it comes to Fieros. He does have tendency to come direct, but I think in this case a couple of happy smilies would have changed the whole intent, so here they are... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Hope this smooths things out. It actually was kinda funny, but I know what it is like to get caught up in a misunderstanding. Take the advice of someone who has tread that road 2 many times, let it slide.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phaeton||05-04-2000||02:18 AM||||Of course color is of primary importance.  Since it is to be painted anyway it would be a shame to have colors that clash.||||reg||12
